ToolFence is a defensive policy proxy for Model Context Protocol (MCP) tool calls.

Dual-use functionality

ToolFence launches user-configured local MCP server processes and mediates requests that may access files, execute shell or Git commands, or make HTTP requests. These general-purpose capabilities can affect arbitrary resources when a user deliberately configures an upstream server and policy to permit them. Process spawning, command inspection, local IPC, and network-action classification may resemble behavior found in offensive or malicious software when examined without context.

Intended legitimate use

ToolFence is intended to help developers reduce accidental or prompt-injected tool misuse. It evaluates normalized actions against explicit allow, deny, or approval rules; fails closed on unknown or ambiguous actions; and records privacy-conscious audit metadata. It does not include exploits, credential theft, persistence mechanisms, evasion features, or autonomous targeting.

Security boundary

ToolFence mediates calls that cross its stdio proxy. It is not an operating-system sandbox and cannot prevent an upstream MCP server process from directly using permissions already granted to that process. Users must only run MCP servers they trust at the operating-system level and should combine ToolFence with process, filesystem, environment, and network isolation where appropriate.
